1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:05,112
Over the summer of 2026, Wall Street analysts raised their estimates of what

2
00:00:05,112 --> 00:00:09,950
S&P 500 companies will earn over the next twelve months by more than nine

3
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:11,038
percent.

4
00:00:11,038 --> 00:00:13,000
The stock market's response?

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,850
For four months, the index went essentially nowhere.

6
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:21,400
If investors had kept paying the same price for each dollar of expected earnings,

7
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,037
the index would have climbed along this line.

8
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:29,087
Instead, the price they're willing to pay, the multiple, shrank, and swallowed

9
00:00:29,087 --> 00:00:32,100
most of the earnings gain.

10
00:00:32,242 --> 00:00:35,979
And underneath that calm surface, something stranger was happening.

11
00:00:35,979 --> 00:00:38,904
Here are the stocks in the S&P 500.

12
00:00:38,904 --> 00:00:42,967
A stock is lit if its price is above its own average of the past fifty trading

13
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:46,604
days, a simple sign that it has been rising lately.

14
00:00:46,604 --> 00:00:51,454
In early October, only about one in four were lit, even though the index itself

15
00:00:51,454 --> 00:00:54,679
sat within a fraction of a percent of its record high.

16
00:00:54,679 --> 00:00:58,454
The average stock was falling, while the giants at the top held the index

17
00:00:58,454 --> 00:01:00,442
up.

18
00:01:00,575 --> 00:01:04,275
That's the puzzle at the heart of a weekly note by Jurrien Timmer, Fidelity's

19
00:01:04,275 --> 00:01:08,825
director of global macro, for the week of October 5th, 2026.

20
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:10,725
He called it Opposing Forces.

21
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:11,600
His argument:

22
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,625
the market is being pulled apart.

23
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:16,200
On one side, an enormous earnings boom.

24
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,550
On the other, a rising cost of capital:

25
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:22,450
interest rates, driven higher by forces that have little to do with the stock

26
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:23,375
market.

27
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:28,150
And the price sits in the middle, the result of the tug of war.

28
00:01:28,358 --> 00:01:32,171
In this video, we'll unpack that argument piece by piece, and check each claim

29
00:01:32,171 --> 00:01:33,683
against public data.

30
00:01:33,683 --> 00:01:37,683
First, the simple arithmetic that links stock prices, profits, and interest

31
00:01:37,683 --> 00:01:38,533
rates.

32
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:40,658
Then, why bond yields are rising:

33
00:01:40,658 --> 00:01:43,058
Timmer points to four separate forces.

34
00:01:43,058 --> 00:01:46,058
Who gets hurt, and why the Federal Reserve is stuck.

35
00:01:46,058 --> 00:01:50,133
The earnings boom itself, and why markets rarely pay up for peak growth.

36
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:51,121
And finally:

37
00:01:51,121 --> 00:01:53,933
is this a bubble, and where are we in the cycle?

38
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:54,758
One note:

39
00:01:54,758 --> 00:01:59,633
this explains one strategist's view as of early October 2026.

40
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:01,433
It is not investment advice.

41
00:02:10,398 --> 00:02:14,486
Let's start with the arithmetic hiding behind every stock-market chart.

42
00:02:14,486 --> 00:02:19,173
Add up the profits of all five hundred companies in the S&P 500 over the past

43
00:02:19,173 --> 00:02:21,436
year, per share of the index.

44
00:02:21,436 --> 00:02:23,086
That's its earnings, E:

45
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:26,073
in June, about 295 dollars.

46
00:02:26,073 --> 00:02:29,861
The index itself traded around 7,450.

47
00:02:29,861 --> 00:02:31,873
That's the price, P.

48
00:02:31,873 --> 00:02:36,386
Divide one by the other and you get the price-to-earnings ratio, the P/E,

49
00:02:36,386 --> 00:02:38,061
also called the multiple:

50
00:02:38,061 --> 00:02:39,636
about 25.

51
00:02:39,636 --> 00:02:44,807
Investors were paying about 25 dollars for every dollar of yearly profit.

52
00:02:44,807 --> 00:02:48,919
Turn that around and you get an identity that is true by definition:

53
00:02:48,919 --> 00:02:51,819
price equals earnings times the multiple.

54
00:02:51,819 --> 00:02:55,782
Every move in the market is some combination of earnings changing and the

55
00:02:55,782 --> 00:02:58,473
multiple changing.

56
00:02:58,473 --> 00:03:00,011
Here's a way to picture it.

57
00:03:00,011 --> 00:03:04,273
Draw a rectangle whose width is earnings and whose height is the multiple.

58
00:03:04,273 --> 00:03:06,298
Its area is the price.

59
00:03:06,298 --> 00:03:11,332
Now let's watch the twelve months to June 2026, month by month.

60
00:03:11,332 --> 00:03:12,507
Earnings surged:

61
00:03:12,507 --> 00:03:14,832
the rectangle got about a third wider.

62
00:03:14,832 --> 00:03:16,307
But it also got shorter:

63
00:03:16,307 --> 00:03:17,819
the multiple shrank.

64
00:03:17,819 --> 00:03:20,307
So the price grew by less than earnings:

65
00:03:20,307 --> 00:03:27,144
earnings times 1.33, multiple times 0.93, price times 1.24.

66
00:03:27,144 --> 00:03:31,548
A great year, but the multiple took back a chunk of the earnings gain.

67
00:03:31,548 --> 00:03:33,873
And the summer was more lopsided still.

68
00:03:33,873 --> 00:03:37,661
From the end of June to the end of September, analysts raised their estimates

69
00:03:37,661 --> 00:03:41,361
of the next twelve months' earnings by 9.3 percent.

70
00:03:41,361 --> 00:03:44,823
The forward P/E fell from 20.4 to 19.

71
00:03:44,823 --> 00:03:47,048
The price rose just two percent.

72
00:03:47,048 --> 00:03:53,048
As Timmer puts it, price is just the residual of the E and the P/E.

73
00:03:53,132 --> 00:03:55,869
One more picture, which we'll come back to.

74
00:03:55,869 --> 00:03:59,244
Put earnings on one axis and the multiple on the other.

75
00:03:59,244 --> 00:04:03,444
Every point on one of these curves has the same price, because earnings times

76
00:04:03,444 --> 00:04:05,932
multiple is the same all along it.

77
00:04:05,932 --> 00:04:08,840
Higher curves mean higher prices.

78
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:11,890
Here's the real path of the S&P 500.

79
00:04:11,890 --> 00:04:16,515
From the bear-market low of October 2022 to the end of 2024, the dot mostly

80
00:04:16,515 --> 00:04:17,528
climbed.

81
00:04:17,528 --> 00:04:21,853
Earnings grew modestly, about 15 percent, but the multiple jumped from about

82
00:04:21,853 --> 00:04:23,428
20 to 29.

83
00:04:23,428 --> 00:04:25,890
That was a rally driven by the multiple.

84
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:27,915
Since then, it's the reverse:

85
00:04:27,915 --> 00:04:33,415
earnings surged, from 210 dollars to 295, while the multiple fell back to

86
00:04:33,415 --> 00:04:35,190
about 25.

87
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:39,815
Both legs carried the price higher, but with completely different engines.

88
00:04:39,815 --> 00:04:42,478
And since June, the story has sharpened.

89
00:04:42,478 --> 00:04:46,753
Earnings estimates kept rising, but the price has gone mostly sideways for

90
00:04:46,753 --> 00:04:47,903
four months.

91
00:04:47,903 --> 00:04:52,278
On this map, the market is sliding along a curve of constant price:

92
00:04:52,278 --> 00:04:55,790
almost every step to the right is cancelled by a step down.

93
00:04:55,790 --> 00:04:59,328
Something is pulling the multiple down nearly as fast as earnings push it

94
00:04:59,328 --> 00:05:00,053
up.

95
00:05:00,053 --> 00:05:03,440
To see what, we need to understand where the multiple comes from.

96
00:05:07,695 --> 00:05:11,520
So why would investors pay less for every dollar of earnings, just as those

97
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:12,858
earnings boom?

98
00:05:12,858 --> 00:05:16,995
To answer that, we need to ask what a share of stock actually is.

99
00:05:16,995 --> 00:05:19,583
It's a claim on the company's future profits:

100
00:05:19,583 --> 00:05:22,883
this year's, next year's, and every year's after that.

101
00:05:22,883 --> 00:05:26,745
Let's draw them as bars, growing a little each year.

102
00:05:26,745 --> 00:05:30,583
But a dollar you'll receive in ten years isn't worth a dollar today.

103
00:05:30,583 --> 00:05:35,120
A dollar today could be invested, say in a Treasury bond, and grow.

104
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,983
So every future dollar has to be discounted:

105
00:05:37,983 --> 00:05:42,608
divided by one plus an interest rate, once for every year you have to wait.

106
00:05:42,608 --> 00:05:45,554
Here's what each bar is worth today.

107
00:05:45,554 --> 00:05:47,266
Now stack up what's left.

108
00:05:47,266 --> 00:05:49,591
That stack is what the share is worth today:

109
00:05:49,591 --> 00:05:50,679
its price.

110
00:05:50,679 --> 00:05:53,704
The far future still counts, but less and less.

111
00:05:53,704 --> 00:05:57,854
With these numbers, the whole stack, out to infinity, comes to twenty times

112
00:05:57,854 --> 00:05:59,416
the first year's profit.

113
00:05:59,416 --> 00:06:02,279
A P/E of twenty.

114
00:06:02,412 --> 00:06:05,200
We can add up that infinite stack exactly.

115
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:09,250
Call the first year's payout D, its growth rate g, and the discount rate r.

116
00:06:09,250 --> 00:06:14,062
The price is D over one plus r, plus the next year's payout, discounted twice,

117
00:06:14,062 --> 00:06:15,850
and so on forever.

118
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:19,412
Each term is the one before it, times the same ratio, q.

119
00:06:19,412 --> 00:06:23,787
That's a geometric series, and it collapses to something remarkably simple:

120
00:06:23,787 --> 00:06:26,537
D divided by r minus g.

121
00:06:26,537 --> 00:06:31,387
Divide both sides by earnings, and out comes the P/E ratio itself:

122
00:06:31,387 --> 00:06:34,937
the share of profits paid out, divided by r minus g.

123
00:06:34,937 --> 00:06:39,187
And the discount rate r is the risk-free interest rate, the yield on Treasury

124
00:06:39,187 --> 00:06:44,287
bonds, plus an extra return investors demand for the risk of owning stocks:

125
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:47,487
the equity risk premium.

126
00:06:47,637 --> 00:06:49,512
Here's why this matters so much.

127
00:06:49,512 --> 00:06:53,387
Take a company that pays out all its profits, so its P/E is simply one over

128
00:06:53,387 --> 00:06:54,087
r minus g.

129
00:06:54,087 --> 00:06:56,212
Plot that against the discount rate.

130
00:06:56,212 --> 00:06:59,637
With r minus g at five percent, the P/E is twenty.

131
00:06:59,637 --> 00:07:02,212
Now raise interest rates by just half a point:

132
00:07:02,212 --> 00:07:05,600
the P/E falls to about eighteen, a nine percent drop.

133
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:06,637
A full point:

134
00:07:06,637 --> 00:07:09,537
about seventeen, down seventeen percent.

135
00:07:09,537 --> 00:07:14,412
Because r minus g is a small number, small changes in rates make big changes

136
00:07:14,412 --> 00:07:16,679
in what investors will pay.

137
00:07:16,679 --> 00:07:19,704
It also matters which dollars you're discounting.

138
00:07:19,704 --> 00:07:24,266
When rates rise from nine to ten percent, a dollar due next year loses about

139
00:07:24,266 --> 00:07:26,466
one percent of its value today.

140
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:30,804
A dollar due in thirty years loses about a quarter, because it gets discounted

141
00:07:30,804 --> 00:07:32,391
thirty times over.

142
00:07:32,391 --> 00:07:37,304
Stocks are claims on very distant profits, so they behave like very long-term

143
00:07:37,304 --> 00:07:39,554
bonds.

144
00:07:39,704 --> 00:07:42,754
This is the idea behind a shortcut Timmer leans on:

145
00:07:42,754 --> 00:07:46,829
the Fed Model, which compares what stocks earn with what bonds pay.

146
00:07:46,829 --> 00:07:50,154
Flip the P/E upside down and you get the earnings yield.

147
00:07:50,154 --> 00:07:55,279
At a forward P/E of 19.7, the stock market earns about 5.1 percent of its

148
00:07:55,279 --> 00:07:56,616
price per year.

149
00:07:56,616 --> 00:08:00,904
A ten-year Treasury now pays 5.3 percent, guaranteed.

150
00:08:00,904 --> 00:08:06,204
And inflation-protected Treasuries pay 2.9 percent on top of inflation.

151
00:08:06,204 --> 00:08:07,966
That's stiff competition.

152
00:08:07,966 --> 00:08:12,341
Why take the risks of owning stocks for a 5.1 percent earnings yield, when

153
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:16,829
the government will pay you 5.3 with essentially no risk of default?

154
00:08:16,829 --> 00:08:20,779
You'd only do it if you expect those earnings to keep growing fast, and even

155
00:08:20,779 --> 00:08:23,116
then you won't pay top dollar for them.

156
00:08:23,116 --> 00:08:24,816
In Timmer's words:

157
00:08:24,816 --> 00:08:28,479
ignore the Fed Model at your peril.

158
00:08:28,612 --> 00:08:30,862
We can even check the size of the effect.

159
00:08:30,862 --> 00:08:36,037
Over the third quarter, the ten-year yield rose by about 0.85 percentage points.

160
00:08:36,037 --> 00:08:39,400
Our simple model, with nothing else changing, says that should knock roughly

161
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:40,950
fifteen percent off the P/E.

162
00:08:40,950 --> 00:08:44,137
The market's actual forward P/E fell about seven.

163
00:08:44,137 --> 00:08:47,337
So far, stocks have absorbed only part of the shock:

164
00:08:47,337 --> 00:08:51,337
investors may be counting on faster growth, or accepting a thinner premium

165
00:08:51,337 --> 00:08:52,775
for owning stocks.

166
00:08:52,775 --> 00:08:54,775
Either way, the real question is:

167
00:08:54,775 --> 00:08:56,587
why are interest rates rising?

168
00:09:00,859 --> 00:09:04,709
First, a quick refresher on bonds, because everything that follows runs through

169
00:09:04,709 --> 00:09:05,521
them.

170
00:09:05,521 --> 00:09:09,084
A ten-year Treasury bond pays a fixed amount of interest every year, then

171
00:09:09,084 --> 00:09:10,509
returns your money.

172
00:09:10,509 --> 00:09:14,584
If new bonds pay four percent, this one is worth its full hundred dollars.

173
00:09:14,584 --> 00:09:18,309
But if new bonds start paying five percent, nobody will pay a hundred dollars

174
00:09:18,309 --> 00:09:19,884
for one paying four.

175
00:09:19,884 --> 00:09:24,496
Its price falls, to about 92, until it yields five percent too.

176
00:09:24,496 --> 00:09:27,484
So when yields rise, bond prices fall.

177
00:09:27,484 --> 00:09:31,884
A rising-yield market is a falling bond market.

178
00:09:32,109 --> 00:09:33,296
Now zoom out.

179
00:09:33,296 --> 00:09:36,884
This is the ten-year Treasury yield since the 1960s.

180
00:09:36,884 --> 00:09:42,271
It peaked above fifteen percent in 1981, then fell for four decades, a forty-year

181
00:09:42,271 --> 00:09:46,909
bull market in bonds, bottoming out in 2020 at well under one percent.

182
00:09:46,909 --> 00:09:48,721
Since then it has turned.

183
00:09:48,721 --> 00:09:52,171
Timmer describes rates as being in a secular bear market:

184
00:09:52,171 --> 00:09:56,759
not a blip, but a regime that can last for years.

185
00:09:56,976 --> 00:09:59,088
Zoom in on the last few years.

186
00:09:59,088 --> 00:10:04,351
After the surge of 2022 and 2023, the yield spent almost three years going

187
00:10:04,351 --> 00:10:05,713
back and forth.

188
00:10:05,713 --> 00:10:09,963
Each high was a little lower than the last, and each low a little higher:

189
00:10:09,963 --> 00:10:15,334
a pattern chart-watchers call a triangle, a market coiling up, undecided.

190
00:10:15,334 --> 00:10:20,421
Timmer's rule of thumb, technical analysis 101, is that a triangle usually

191
00:10:20,421 --> 00:10:23,834
breaks in the direction of the trend that came before it, and that trend was

192
00:10:23,834 --> 00:10:24,671
up.

193
00:10:24,671 --> 00:10:28,646
This summer, as the triangle narrowed, the yield broke out, upward.

194
00:10:28,646 --> 00:10:31,796
By early October it was 5.3 percent.

195
00:10:31,796 --> 00:10:36,459
Timmer borrows a line adapted from Hemingway, about how people go bankrupt:

196
00:10:36,459 --> 00:10:39,684
slowly, then all at once.

197
00:10:39,809 --> 00:10:41,321
What kind of rise is this?

198
00:10:41,321 --> 00:10:43,434
A bond's yield has two parts.

199
00:10:43,434 --> 00:10:47,784
The nominal yield is a real yield, what you earn after inflation, plus the

200
00:10:47,784 --> 00:10:50,734
inflation investors expect along the way.

201
00:10:50,734 --> 00:10:55,284
And the Treasury sells inflation-protected bonds, so we can measure both.

202
00:10:55,284 --> 00:10:57,759
Here they are.

203
00:11:00,267 --> 00:11:05,130
Over the past year, the ten-year yield rose by about 1.2 percentage points.

204
00:11:05,130 --> 00:11:08,105
The real yield rose by 1.2 points.

205
00:11:08,105 --> 00:11:09,680
Expected inflation:

206
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:11,380
essentially unchanged.

207
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:13,492
So this is not an inflation scare.

208
00:11:13,492 --> 00:11:17,442
It's the real price of money that's going up.

209
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:20,288
There's a second way to split the ten-year yield.

210
00:11:20,288 --> 00:11:24,351
Part of it is simply what investors expect short-term interest rates, which

211
00:11:24,351 --> 00:11:27,901
the Fed controls, to average over the next ten years.

212
00:11:27,901 --> 00:11:31,376
Because instead of lending for ten years, you could keep lending for three

213
00:11:31,376 --> 00:11:33,913
months at a time and roll it over.

214
00:11:33,913 --> 00:11:36,001
The rest is the term premium:

215
00:11:36,001 --> 00:11:40,338
the extra pay investors demand for locking their money up for ten years, and

216
00:11:40,338 --> 00:11:43,109
bearing whatever happens in between.

217
00:11:43,109 --> 00:11:47,209
The term premium can't be observed directly, but economists at the New York

218
00:11:47,209 --> 00:11:49,146
Fed estimate it with a model.

219
00:11:49,146 --> 00:11:53,296
For most of the 2010s it shrank, and in 2020 it was negative:

220
00:11:53,296 --> 00:11:56,534
investors were paying for the privilege of lending long.

221
00:11:56,534 --> 00:12:01,984
Now it's about 0.9 percent, the 89 basis points Timmer calls a cycle high,

222
00:12:01,984 --> 00:12:04,259
the highest since 2014.

223
00:12:04,259 --> 00:12:09,284
Lenders want to be paid for holding long-term government debt again.

224
00:12:09,434 --> 00:12:12,321
So what's pushing real yields and the term premium up?

225
00:12:12,321 --> 00:12:15,334
Timmer names four forces, all at work at once:

226
00:12:15,334 --> 00:12:18,221
the rising cost of servicing the government's debt;

227
00:12:18,221 --> 00:12:21,759
too few buyers left who buy bonds regardless of price;

228
00:12:21,759 --> 00:12:25,534
what he calls reverse crowding out, from the AI investment boom;

229
00:12:25,534 --> 00:12:28,971
and, most recently, a jolt from the mortgage market.

230
00:12:28,971 --> 00:12:30,659
Let's take them one at a time.

231
00:12:34,923 --> 00:12:36,048
Force number one:

232
00:12:36,048 --> 00:12:37,560
the government's debt.

233
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:41,648
In August, total federal debt passed 40 trillion dollars.

234
00:12:41,648 --> 00:12:47,198
At the end of 2019 it was 23 trillion, so it has grown by about 17 trillion

235
00:12:47,198 --> 00:12:50,148
dollars since COVID.

236
00:12:50,273 --> 00:12:53,685
But Timmer's point is that the size of the debt is old news.

237
00:12:53,685 --> 00:12:56,010
What's new is the cost of carrying it.

238
00:12:56,010 --> 00:12:59,348
Here's what the government pays in interest each year, as a share of the whole

239
00:12:59,348 --> 00:13:00,485
economy.

240
00:13:00,485 --> 00:13:05,473
In 2021, with rates near zero, it was about two and a third percent.

241
00:13:05,473 --> 00:13:09,735
Now it's about four percent, the highest since the late 1990s.

242
00:13:09,735 --> 00:13:14,573
Interest is now one of the biggest items in the federal budget.

243
00:13:14,698 --> 00:13:18,410
Why does the cost keep climbing even when rates stop rising?

244
00:13:18,410 --> 00:13:22,685
Picture the debt as a stack of bonds, each locked in at the rate that applied

245
00:13:22,685 --> 00:13:24,048
when it was sold.

246
00:13:24,048 --> 00:13:26,610
Many were sold when rates were near zero.

247
00:13:26,610 --> 00:13:30,823
But bonds mature, and the government has to borrow again to repay them, at

248
00:13:30,823 --> 00:13:32,456
today's rate.

249
00:13:32,456 --> 00:13:37,444
So one by one, cheap old bonds get replaced by expensive new ones, and the

250
00:13:37,444 --> 00:13:41,281
average rate on the whole stack drifts up toward the market rate.

251
00:13:41,281 --> 00:13:45,456
That takes years, which means the interest bill keeps growing long after market

252
00:13:45,456 --> 00:13:48,381
rates stop rising.

253
00:13:48,498 --> 00:13:49,873
Here's the real thing.

254
00:13:49,873 --> 00:13:54,148
The average rate the government pays on its debt bottomed at under two percent

255
00:13:54,148 --> 00:13:55,498
in 2020.

256
00:13:55,498 --> 00:13:57,073
It's now above three.

257
00:13:57,073 --> 00:14:00,760
And the market rate for new ten-year borrowing is above five.

258
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:05,848
The gap between the two lines is cost that hasn't arrived yet.

259
00:14:05,998 --> 00:14:07,910
And this can feed on itself.

260
00:14:07,910 --> 00:14:10,410
Higher rates mean a bigger interest bill.

261
00:14:10,410 --> 00:14:14,973
A bigger interest bill means bigger deficits, which means more bonds to sell,

262
00:14:14,973 --> 00:14:18,248
and selling more bonds pushes rates higher still.

263
00:14:18,248 --> 00:14:22,785
With the deficit already around six percent of GDP in an economy that isn't

264
00:14:22,785 --> 00:14:27,023
in recession, bond investors notice.

265
00:14:27,165 --> 00:14:31,352
There's a simple piece of arithmetic that tells you when debt becomes a problem.

266
00:14:31,352 --> 00:14:34,952
What matters isn't the debt itself, but the debt compared with the size of

267
00:14:34,952 --> 00:14:37,202
the economy that has to carry it.

268
00:14:37,202 --> 00:14:41,340
Each year, the debt grows with the interest rate r, plus any new borrowing

269
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:45,290
beyond the interest, while the economy grows at its own rate, g.

270
00:14:45,290 --> 00:14:49,140
Work through the division, and the change in the debt ratio comes out as r

271
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:53,898
minus g, times the debt, plus the deficit before interest.

272
00:14:53,898 --> 00:14:56,323
Everything hinges on that first term.

273
00:14:56,323 --> 00:15:00,160
When the interest rate is below the growth rate, debt shrinks relative to

274
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:02,423
the economy all by itself.

275
00:15:02,423 --> 00:15:07,185
When it's above, the debt snowballs, even if the rest of the budget balances.

276
00:15:07,185 --> 00:15:08,998
Hold on to r minus g:

277
00:15:08,998 --> 00:15:10,573
in a few minutes we'll measure it.

278
00:15:14,854 --> 00:15:17,866
Force number two is about who buys all these bonds.

279
00:15:17,866 --> 00:15:20,504
Timmer's term is non-economic buyers:

280
00:15:20,504 --> 00:15:23,654
investors who buy bonds for reasons other than the return.

281
00:15:23,654 --> 00:15:27,241
The Federal Reserve, which bought trillions of dollars of Treasuries to push

282
00:15:27,241 --> 00:15:28,854
long-term rates down.

283
00:15:28,854 --> 00:15:32,304
Foreign central banks, which park their reserves in Treasuries.

284
00:15:32,304 --> 00:15:36,329
And banks and insurers that hold them because regulations require it.

285
00:15:36,329 --> 00:15:40,179
None of them haggle much over price, so as long as they're buying, yields

286
00:15:40,179 --> 00:15:43,604
stay lower than they otherwise would.

287
00:15:43,762 --> 00:15:45,850
Here's who has owned the government's debt.

288
00:15:45,850 --> 00:15:48,250
In teal, the share owned by the Fed.

289
00:15:48,250 --> 00:15:52,675
In blue, the share owned by foreign investors, including foreign central banks.

290
00:15:52,675 --> 00:15:55,975
Together they peaked at about seventy percent in 2014.

291
00:15:55,975 --> 00:15:58,187
Today it's about forty-five percent.

292
00:15:58,187 --> 00:16:01,887
The Fed spent three and a half years shrinking its bond holdings, and even

293
00:16:01,887 --> 00:16:06,437
now that it's buying again, mostly short-term bills, its share keeps falling.

294
00:16:06,437 --> 00:16:10,421
Foreign buyers haven't kept pace with all the new debt either.

295
00:16:10,421 --> 00:16:14,471
In Timmer's words, central banks are taking the punch bowl away rather than

296
00:16:14,471 --> 00:16:15,583
spiking it.

297
00:16:15,583 --> 00:16:19,008
So more and more of the debt has to be sold to everyone else:

298
00:16:19,008 --> 00:16:23,021
pension funds, asset managers, hedge funds, households.

299
00:16:23,021 --> 00:16:25,533
These buyers do care about price.

300
00:16:25,533 --> 00:16:29,483
To take on more long-term government debt, they demand a better deal:

301
00:16:29,483 --> 00:16:32,621
a higher term premium.

302
00:16:32,787 --> 00:16:33,900
One twist.

303
00:16:33,900 --> 00:16:37,712
After years of losses, you might expect ordinary investors to be dumping their

304
00:16:37,712 --> 00:16:38,975
bond funds.

305
00:16:38,975 --> 00:16:41,750
Timmer's fund-flow data shows the opposite:

306
00:16:41,750 --> 00:16:45,862
end investors have been buying bonds through ETFs and mutual funds about as

307
00:16:45,862 --> 00:16:48,087
fast as they've been buying stocks.

308
00:16:48,087 --> 00:16:49,225
As he puts it:

309
00:16:49,225 --> 00:16:52,137
someone is selling, but it's not mom and pop.

310
00:16:52,137 --> 00:16:56,112
The pressure is coming from the big, price-blind buyers stepping back, and

311
00:16:56,112 --> 00:16:58,062
from the sheer volume of supply.

312
00:17:02,319 --> 00:17:05,707
Force number three is about the world's supply of savings.

313
00:17:05,707 --> 00:17:07,644
Think of interest rates as a price:

314
00:17:07,644 --> 00:17:09,694
the price of borrowing savings.

315
00:17:09,694 --> 00:17:12,194
Savers lend more when they're paid more.

316
00:17:12,194 --> 00:17:16,694
Borrowers, governments and companies, borrow more when money is cheap.

317
00:17:16,694 --> 00:17:20,494
Where the two curves cross sets the real interest rate.

318
00:17:20,494 --> 00:17:25,094
Through the 2000s and 2010s, the world had what Ben Bernanke called a savings

319
00:17:25,094 --> 00:17:25,957
glut:

320
00:17:25,957 --> 00:17:30,894
a flood of savings from fast-growing Asian economies, oil exporters, and aging

321
00:17:30,894 --> 00:17:34,657
rich societies, all looking for safe places to go.

322
00:17:34,657 --> 00:17:38,357
The supply curve shifted out, and the price of money fell.

323
00:17:38,357 --> 00:17:43,486
That glut is a big part of why bond yields stayed so low for so long.

324
00:17:43,486 --> 00:17:47,061
Timmer argues that glut has turned into a savings shortage.

325
00:17:47,061 --> 00:17:51,086
On the borrowing side, governments are running big deficits, and companies

326
00:17:51,086 --> 00:17:54,698
are pouring money into building out artificial intelligence.

327
00:17:54,698 --> 00:17:59,111
Demand for savings shifts out, while, in his telling, the surplus savings

328
00:17:59,111 --> 00:18:01,498
that once flooded in have dried up.

329
00:18:01,498 --> 00:18:03,148
The crossing point moves up:

330
00:18:03,148 --> 00:18:06,436
a higher real interest rate.

331
00:18:06,619 --> 00:18:09,282
Economists have long worried about crowding out:

332
00:18:09,282 --> 00:18:13,569
heavy government borrowing pushes up rates, and private companies cancel projects

333
00:18:13,569 --> 00:18:15,232
that no longer pay.

334
00:18:15,232 --> 00:18:18,032
Timmer's twist is reverse crowding out.

335
00:18:18,032 --> 00:18:21,682
Today it's the private sector competing with the government for savings.

336
00:18:21,682 --> 00:18:26,032
The companies building AI expect returns far above what they pay to borrow,

337
00:18:26,032 --> 00:18:28,194
so higher rates don't stop them.

338
00:18:28,194 --> 00:18:33,519
Someone has to give way, and it's the price the Treasury pays to borrow.

339
00:18:33,694 --> 00:18:35,232
How big is that build-out?

340
00:18:35,232 --> 00:18:38,982
Here's American business investment in information processing equipment and

341
00:18:38,982 --> 00:18:41,669
software, as a share of the economy.

342
00:18:41,669 --> 00:18:45,307
It's a broad measure, but it's where the servers, chips and software of the

343
00:18:45,307 --> 00:18:46,994
AI boom show up.

344
00:18:46,994 --> 00:18:50,407
At the height of the dot-com boom it reached about four and a half percent

345
00:18:50,407 --> 00:18:51,669
of GDP.

346
00:18:51,669 --> 00:18:53,607
It's now above five percent:

347
00:18:53,607 --> 00:18:58,132
about 1.6 trillion dollars a year, competing for the same pool of savings

348
00:18:58,132 --> 00:18:59,169
as the Treasury.

349
00:19:03,417 --> 00:19:07,117
Force number four comes from an odd feature of American home loans.

350
00:19:07,117 --> 00:19:10,742
The typical mortgage has a fixed rate for thirty years, and the homeowner

351
00:19:10,742 --> 00:19:14,017
can pay it off early, any time, with no penalty.

352
00:19:14,017 --> 00:19:16,555
So when rates fall, people refinance.

353
00:19:16,555 --> 00:19:20,642
Those mortgages are bundled into bonds, mortgage-backed securities, owned

354
00:19:20,642 --> 00:19:22,917
by banks, funds and the Fed.

355
00:19:22,917 --> 00:19:26,255
And the homeowner's right to refinance is an option that the bond's owner

356
00:19:26,255 --> 00:19:29,717
has, in effect, sold them.

357
00:19:29,826 --> 00:19:32,038
Here's what that option does to the bond.

358
00:19:32,038 --> 00:19:32,826
In grey:

359
00:19:32,826 --> 00:19:36,876
what a pool of six percent mortgages would be worth if nobody could refinance.

360
00:19:36,876 --> 00:19:40,488
When rates fall, its price climbs, and the curve bends upward, which is a

361
00:19:40,488 --> 00:19:42,876
pleasant property called convexity.

362
00:19:42,876 --> 00:19:44,126
Now the real thing.

363
00:19:44,126 --> 00:19:48,251
When rates fall, homeowners refinance and pay the loans back at face value,

364
00:19:48,251 --> 00:19:50,551
so the bond's price can barely rise.

365
00:19:50,551 --> 00:19:54,326
When rates rise, nobody refinances, and the price falls.

366
00:19:54,326 --> 00:19:56,801
The curve bends the wrong way.

367
00:19:56,801 --> 00:19:58,713
That's negative convexity:

368
00:19:58,713 --> 00:20:01,967
you lose on the way down and don't win on the way up.

369
00:20:01,967 --> 00:20:05,380
The slope of that curve is what bond investors call duration:

370
00:20:05,380 --> 00:20:07,692
how sensitive the price is to rates.

371
00:20:07,692 --> 00:20:11,980
When rates are low, the mortgage bond behaves like a short-term bond, because

372
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:14,692
everyone is expected to refinance soon.

373
00:20:14,692 --> 00:20:19,117
But as rates rise past the mortgage rate, refinancing stops, the loans are

374
00:20:19,117 --> 00:20:23,467
expected to last much longer, and the duration stretches out, from about one

375
00:20:23,467 --> 00:20:25,280
year to more than six.

376
00:20:25,280 --> 00:20:30,692
Owners suddenly hold far more interest-rate risk than they signed up for.

377
00:20:30,859 --> 00:20:33,071
And that creates another feedback loop.

378
00:20:33,071 --> 00:20:34,259
Rates rise.

379
00:20:34,259 --> 00:20:36,684
Mortgage bonds' duration stretches out.

380
00:20:36,684 --> 00:20:40,434
The big holders, and the companies that service mortgages, often hedge their

381
00:20:40,434 --> 00:20:44,209
risk, so to get back to their target they sell Treasuries, or make equivalent

382
00:20:44,209 --> 00:20:46,021
trades in the swap market.

383
00:20:46,021 --> 00:20:50,609
That selling pushes Treasury prices down, which means yields go up further.

384
00:20:50,609 --> 00:20:52,471
Rate moves get amplified.

385
00:20:52,471 --> 00:20:56,971
This is called convexity hedging, and it has turned modest bond selloffs into

386
00:20:56,971 --> 00:20:59,759
sharp ones before.

387
00:20:59,892 --> 00:21:01,080
Here's the trigger.

388
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:05,142
Early this year, mortgage rates dipped to about six percent, the lowest in

389
00:21:05,142 --> 00:21:06,667
more than three years.

390
00:21:06,667 --> 00:21:11,230
By October first they were back above seven, the highest since 2023.

391
00:21:11,230 --> 00:21:16,355
A swing like that, over a few months, is exactly what sets off convexity hedging.

392
00:21:16,355 --> 00:21:19,605
Timmer counts it as the most recent of the four forces:

393
00:21:19,605 --> 00:21:22,592
a negative convexity event from the mortgage market.

394
00:21:26,824 --> 00:21:30,762
There's a more comforting explanation for rising real yields, and Timmer takes

395
00:21:30,762 --> 00:21:31,974
it seriously:

396
00:21:31,974 --> 00:21:36,749
maybe the economy is simply running hot, so money is in demand for good reasons.

397
00:21:36,749 --> 00:21:40,537
But apart from strong surveys of purchasing managers, he doesn't see it in

398
00:21:40,537 --> 00:21:41,624
the data.

399
00:21:41,624 --> 00:21:43,399
Real growth over the past year:

400
00:21:43,399 --> 00:21:45,412
about 2.2 percent.

401
00:21:45,412 --> 00:21:50,087
The Congressional Budget Office's estimate of the economy's long-run potential:

402
00:21:50,087 --> 00:21:52,399
also about 2.2.

403
00:21:52,399 --> 00:21:57,474
And economists surveyed by Bloomberg expect 2.1 to 2.2 percent a year through

404
00:21:57,474 --> 00:21:58,887
2028.

405
00:21:58,887 --> 00:21:59,762
Decent.

406
00:21:59,762 --> 00:22:02,199
Not booming.

407
00:22:02,324 --> 00:22:04,974
Remember r minus g, from the debt arithmetic?

408
00:22:04,974 --> 00:22:05,899
Let's measure it:

409
00:22:05,899 --> 00:22:09,549
the real yield on ten-year Treasuries, against the economy's potential growth

410
00:22:09,549 --> 00:22:10,224
rate.

411
00:22:10,224 --> 00:22:15,237
From about 2011 to 2025, r sat below g, often far below.

412
00:22:15,237 --> 00:22:19,449
That's the world where government debt quietly shrinks relative to the economy.

413
00:22:19,449 --> 00:22:23,824
Now r is about 2.9 percent, and g is about 2.2.

414
00:22:23,824 --> 00:22:27,924
For the first time since 2010, the real cost of borrowing for ten years is

415
00:22:27,924 --> 00:22:30,391
above the economy's growth rate.

416
00:22:30,391 --> 00:22:34,466
The government still pays a lower average rate on its older debt, so this

417
00:22:34,466 --> 00:22:36,266
isn't an emergency today.

418
00:22:36,266 --> 00:22:40,616
But every new dollar it borrows now costs more, after inflation, than the

419
00:22:40,616 --> 00:22:43,103
economy is expected to grow.

420
00:22:43,103 --> 00:22:48,366
Timmer calls that a warning sign for both the budget and for growth.

421
00:22:48,499 --> 00:22:52,699
If fiscal risk is the story, you'd expect bond markets to punish weak government

422
00:22:52,699 --> 00:22:54,212
balance sheets most.

423
00:22:54,212 --> 00:22:56,537
Timmer's exhibit A is France.

424
00:22:56,537 --> 00:23:00,887
French ten-year yields are around four percent, the highest since 2008, and

425
00:23:00,887 --> 00:23:04,149
France now pays about eight-tenths of a point more than Germany to borrow

426
00:23:04,149 --> 00:23:08,662
for ten years, a gap that was usually far narrower over the past decade.

427
00:23:08,662 --> 00:23:12,199
The same rising rates hurt anyone with a weak balance sheet:

428
00:23:12,199 --> 00:23:17,699
indebted governments, lower-quality companies, and most consumers.

429
00:23:17,858 --> 00:23:19,308
So Timmer's verdict:

430
00:23:19,308 --> 00:23:23,683
the main reason real yields are rising is not a booming economy.

431
00:23:23,683 --> 00:23:28,795
It's fiscal risk, amplified by AI's appetite for capital and by convexity

432
00:23:28,795 --> 00:23:30,745
hedging in the mortgage market.

433
00:23:30,745 --> 00:23:34,645
That's a less comfortable reason for rates to rise, because it doesn't come

434
00:23:34,645 --> 00:23:36,583
with faster growth to pay for it.

435
00:23:40,848 --> 00:23:42,735
So what can the Federal Reserve do?

436
00:23:42,735 --> 00:23:44,398
Here's its policy rate.

437
00:23:44,398 --> 00:23:49,648
It raised rates hard in 2022 and 2023, then started cutting.

438
00:23:49,648 --> 00:23:55,823
In late 2025 it cut three more times, and last month, in September 2026, it

439
00:23:55,823 --> 00:23:58,756
reversed course and raised rates again.

440
00:23:58,756 --> 00:24:00,768
The bond market wants more.

441
00:24:00,768 --> 00:24:04,668
The two-year Treasury yield, which roughly tracks where investors expect the

442
00:24:04,668 --> 00:24:09,243
Fed's rate to be over the next couple of years, is about 4.8 percent:

443
00:24:09,243 --> 00:24:11,606
well above the Fed's current range.

444
00:24:11,606 --> 00:24:14,256
Timmer reads the market's message bluntly:

445
00:24:14,256 --> 00:24:18,781
the Fed shouldn't have eased in 2025, and now it needs to take those cuts

446
00:24:18,781 --> 00:24:20,881
back.

447
00:24:20,973 --> 00:24:24,523
But the Fed has one blunt instrument, and many different borrowers on the

448
00:24:24,523 --> 00:24:25,735
other end of it.

449
00:24:25,735 --> 00:24:29,323
The government can keep pushing its problem down the road, as long as the

450
00:24:29,323 --> 00:24:30,985
bond market lets it.

451
00:24:30,985 --> 00:24:32,273
Homebuyers can't:

452
00:24:32,273 --> 00:24:35,173
they face mortgage rates above seven percent.

453
00:24:35,173 --> 00:24:38,648
Neither can weaker companies that need to refinance their debt.

454
00:24:38,648 --> 00:24:41,098
And the AI giants barely notice:

455
00:24:41,098 --> 00:24:44,760
the returns they expect on what they borrow are far above the Fed's price

456
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:46,173
of money.

457
00:24:46,173 --> 00:24:50,273
Raise rates enough to satisfy the bond market, and you crush the borrowers

458
00:24:50,273 --> 00:24:54,685
who are already struggling, while barely touching the ones driving the boom.

459
00:24:54,685 --> 00:24:57,348
That's why Timmer describes the Fed as stuck.

460
00:25:01,588 --> 00:25:05,376
Back to the stock market, and the strange calm we started with.

461
00:25:05,376 --> 00:25:10,738
The S&P 500 that everyone quotes weights each company by its size, its total

462
00:25:10,738 --> 00:25:11,938
market value.

463
00:25:11,938 --> 00:25:16,401
So a handful of trillion-dollar giants carry a big share of the index.

464
00:25:16,401 --> 00:25:20,713
There's also an equal-weighted version, where every company counts the same.

465
00:25:20,713 --> 00:25:25,163
It's a good gauge of how the average stock is doing.

466
00:25:25,288 --> 00:25:27,951
Here are the two versions in 2026.

467
00:25:27,951 --> 00:25:30,588
In blue, the familiar cap-weighted index;

468
00:25:30,588 --> 00:25:32,763
in gold, the equal-weighted one.

469
00:25:32,763 --> 00:25:37,113
From the end of June to early October, the big index rose about four percent.

470
00:25:37,113 --> 00:25:38,288
The average stock:

471
00:25:38,288 --> 00:25:39,613
down slightly.

472
00:25:39,613 --> 00:25:40,826
Timmer's reading:

473
00:25:40,826 --> 00:25:44,338
higher borrowing costs are starting to bite the weaker companies, while the

474
00:25:44,338 --> 00:25:48,813
trillion-dollar club shrugs it off, so far, at least.

475
00:25:48,955 --> 00:25:50,830
Timmer measures this with breadth:

476
00:25:50,830 --> 00:25:54,555
the share of stocks trading above their own recent average price.

477
00:25:54,555 --> 00:25:56,442
On top, the index.

478
00:25:56,442 --> 00:26:00,380
Below, computed from the daily prices of all the index's members:

479
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:05,167
the share above their fifty-day average, and above their two-hundred-day average.

480
00:26:05,167 --> 00:26:08,930
By October 2nd, only about a quarter of stocks were above their fifty-day

481
00:26:08,930 --> 00:26:12,992
average, and fewer than half above their two-hundred-day, while the index

482
00:26:12,992 --> 00:26:14,921
sat near a record.

483
00:26:14,921 --> 00:26:16,271
Timmer's phrase for it:

484
00:26:16,271 --> 00:26:17,746
a stealth correction.

485
00:26:17,746 --> 00:26:21,134
The rising cost of money has the indexes in a vise.

486
00:26:21,134 --> 00:26:25,159
The headline number holds steady, while underneath, most stocks are already

487
00:26:25,159 --> 00:26:27,371
falling.

488
00:26:27,496 --> 00:26:28,696
There's one more wrinkle:

489
00:26:28,696 --> 00:26:29,834
the calendar.

490
00:26:29,834 --> 00:26:34,159
Since 1950, measured by monthly average prices, the stretch from September

491
00:26:34,159 --> 00:26:38,159
into October has been the weakest of the year, and November and December among

492
00:26:38,159 --> 00:26:39,459
the strongest.

493
00:26:39,459 --> 00:26:43,396
Timmer notes that this seasonally weak stretch ends in about two weeks, just

494
00:26:43,396 --> 00:26:45,496
as earnings season gets going.

495
00:26:45,496 --> 00:26:49,209
If the pressure from rates eases, he thinks the indexes could easily pick

496
00:26:49,209 --> 00:26:50,546
up speed again.

497
00:26:50,546 --> 00:26:52,284
But for now, as he puts it:

498
00:26:52,284 --> 00:26:54,271
ignore the Fed Model at your peril.

499
00:26:58,570 --> 00:27:00,433
Now the other side of the rope.

500
00:27:00,433 --> 00:27:06,270
Here's S&P 500 earnings growth, quarter by quarter, compared with a year earlier.

501
00:27:06,270 --> 00:27:09,008
Last year, a healthy thirteen percent.

502
00:27:09,008 --> 00:27:10,220
Then it took off:

503
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:15,570
about 28 percent in the first quarter of 2026, and 52 percent in the second,

504
00:27:15,570 --> 00:27:18,403
the fastest since 2021.

505
00:27:18,403 --> 00:27:21,453
And notice how companies keep beating expectations.

506
00:27:21,453 --> 00:27:25,391
The dashed lines show what analysts expected when each quarter began:

507
00:27:25,391 --> 00:27:29,066
about thirteen percent for the first quarter, which came in at 28;

508
00:27:29,066 --> 00:27:32,541
23 percent for the second, which came in at 52.

509
00:27:32,541 --> 00:27:37,041
For the third quarter, the one about to be reported, analysts now expect about

510
00:27:37,041 --> 00:27:38,353
30 percent.

511
00:27:38,353 --> 00:27:42,566
If the usual earnings-season bounce of recent quarters happens again, Timmer

512
00:27:42,566 --> 00:27:46,353
figures it could come in at 30 to 35 percent.

513
00:27:46,353 --> 00:27:50,278
Profit margins are at a record, and a record number of companies raised their

514
00:27:50,278 --> 00:27:52,328
guidance going into the quarter.

515
00:27:52,328 --> 00:27:53,428
Timmer's line:

516
00:27:53,428 --> 00:27:55,991
who wants to step in front of a train like that?

517
00:27:55,991 --> 00:27:59,028
Then again, look at the estimates further out.

518
00:27:59,028 --> 00:28:03,553
Growth is expected to fade, to near zero by the middle of next year, partly

519
00:28:03,553 --> 00:28:07,303
because the comparisons get so tough.

520
00:28:07,537 --> 00:28:10,299
Zoom out, and earnings growth comes in waves.

521
00:28:10,299 --> 00:28:14,024
Here's the growth of trailing twelve-month earnings since 2012.

522
00:28:14,024 --> 00:28:17,224
Timmer sees a roughly four-year rhythm in these waves:

523
00:28:17,224 --> 00:28:22,037
crests around 2014, 2018, 2021, and now.

524
00:28:22,037 --> 00:28:26,087
Right now, all the waves he tracks are still rising, and all are in double

525
00:28:26,087 --> 00:28:28,312
digits.

526
00:28:28,458 --> 00:28:32,433
To see where a wave is heading, Timmer looks at its derivatives, exactly as

527
00:28:32,433 --> 00:28:33,808
in calculus.

528
00:28:33,808 --> 00:28:37,883
If earnings growth swings up and down like a wave, then its rate of change,

529
00:28:37,883 --> 00:28:42,233
how fast growth itself is speeding up, is another wave, shifted earlier by

530
00:28:42,233 --> 00:28:43,758
a quarter of a cycle.

531
00:28:43,758 --> 00:28:45,945
The acceleration peaks first.

532
00:28:45,945 --> 00:28:49,720
Growth keeps rising for a while after that, but at a slowing pace, and then

533
00:28:49,720 --> 00:28:51,191
it crests.

534
00:28:51,191 --> 00:28:54,503
Timmer says a peak in that second derivative is due.

535
00:28:54,503 --> 00:28:55,828
In other words:

536
00:28:55,828 --> 00:29:00,541
earnings are still growing fast, and will probably keep growing, but the acceleration

537
00:29:00,541 --> 00:29:04,816
is about to turn, and with it, the market's sense that things can only get

538
00:29:04,816 --> 00:29:06,941
better.

539
00:29:07,124 --> 00:29:09,437
Here's why that matters for the multiple.

540
00:29:09,437 --> 00:29:13,824
Take every month since 1960, sort them by how fast earnings grew over the

541
00:29:13,824 --> 00:29:17,774
previous year, and look at what happened to the P/E over the same year.

542
00:29:17,774 --> 00:29:21,499
When earnings were shrinking, the multiple usually expanded.

543
00:29:21,499 --> 00:29:25,562
When earnings grew more than thirty percent, the P/E typically fell by about

544
00:29:25,562 --> 00:29:26,662
a quarter.

545
00:29:26,662 --> 00:29:31,199
As Timmer puts it, investors don't usually pay top multiples for peak earnings

546
00:29:31,199 --> 00:29:32,470
growth.

547
00:29:32,470 --> 00:29:34,208
Part of this is mechanical:

548
00:29:34,208 --> 00:29:38,470
stock prices look ahead, so they tend to rise before the earnings show up,

549
00:29:38,470 --> 00:29:41,283
and the multiple shrinks as earnings catch up.

550
00:29:41,283 --> 00:29:45,295
Either way, with trailing earnings up around thirty percent and the next twelve

551
00:29:45,295 --> 00:29:49,495
months expected to add another twenty, a falling multiple is what history

552
00:29:49,495 --> 00:29:50,733
would predict.

553
00:29:50,733 --> 00:29:56,270
Timmer's trailing P/E is down about ten percent from a year ago.

554
00:29:56,445 --> 00:29:58,970
Timmer sees the same pattern abroad.

555
00:29:58,970 --> 00:30:03,408
Five-year growth rates of earnings, and of what companies pay out to shareholders,

556
00:30:03,408 --> 00:30:05,495
look to be peaking, in the U.S.

557
00:30:05,495 --> 00:30:06,783
and globally.

558
00:30:06,783 --> 00:30:09,820
Japan and Canada stand out as the winners.

559
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:10,258
U.S.

560
00:30:10,258 --> 00:30:14,370
payouts are growing about ten percent a year, which he calls hardly compelling

561
00:30:14,370 --> 00:30:15,520
by comparison.

562
00:30:19,584 --> 00:30:20,747
So is this a bubble?

563
00:30:20,747 --> 00:30:22,059
Timmer's answer:

564
00:30:22,059 --> 00:30:26,122
whatever this cycle is, it isn't a bubble, because bubbles are about valuation.

565
00:30:26,122 --> 00:30:27,447
Remember this map.

566
00:30:27,447 --> 00:30:31,659
A bubble is when the price climbs because the multiple keeps expanding, investors

567
00:30:31,659 --> 00:30:34,447
paying more and more for each dollar of profit.

568
00:30:34,447 --> 00:30:37,759
That's roughly what happened in the first two years of this bull market, when

569
00:30:37,759 --> 00:30:40,284
multiples soared while earnings lagged.

570
00:30:40,284 --> 00:30:42,134
Lately, it's been the reverse:

571
00:30:42,134 --> 00:30:46,359
earnings doing the work, the multiple shrinking.

572
00:30:46,459 --> 00:30:50,397
By the usual yardstick, prices relative to the next twelve months of expected

573
00:30:50,397 --> 00:30:53,584
earnings, valuations look unremarkable.

574
00:30:53,584 --> 00:30:59,297
FactSet's forward P/E has fallen from 21.5 in February to 19, right around

575
00:30:59,297 --> 00:31:01,034
its ten-year average.

576
00:31:01,034 --> 00:31:05,809
Timmer puts the cap-weighted index at 19.7 times forward earnings, and the

577
00:31:05,809 --> 00:31:09,251
equal-weighted one at just 17.3.

578
00:31:09,251 --> 00:31:12,413
To be fair, not every measure is so relaxed.

579
00:31:12,413 --> 00:31:16,326
Shiller's CAPE, which compares prices with ten years of inflation-adjusted

580
00:31:16,326 --> 00:31:21,901
earnings, is above 40, not far below its 2000 peak, partly because recent

581
00:31:21,901 --> 00:31:26,576
earnings have grown so fast that a ten-year average lags far behind them.

582
00:31:26,576 --> 00:31:30,588
Which measure you trust matters, and that's why Timmer checks the Fed Model

583
00:31:30,588 --> 00:31:33,476
three different ways.

584
00:31:33,592 --> 00:31:34,905
The Fed Model again:

585
00:31:34,905 --> 00:31:37,505
stocks' earnings yield minus the bond yield.

586
00:31:37,505 --> 00:31:39,667
Above zero, stocks pay more than bonds;

587
00:31:39,667 --> 00:31:41,080
below zero, less.

588
00:31:41,080 --> 00:31:43,792
Using forward earnings, the gap is roughly zero:

589
00:31:43,792 --> 00:31:46,905
about 5.1 percent against 5.3.

590
00:31:46,905 --> 00:31:50,567
Using the past year's earnings, it's about minus 1.4 points:

591
00:31:50,567 --> 00:31:56,255
the lowest since 2002, setting aside 2009, when earnings briefly collapsed.

592
00:31:56,255 --> 00:32:00,667
The real danger zones were 1987 and 2000.

593
00:32:00,667 --> 00:32:04,767
And the measure Shiller computes, from CAPE and real yields, is down to about

594
00:32:04,767 --> 00:32:08,942
half a point, also its lowest since 2002.

595
00:32:08,942 --> 00:32:12,717
So, depending on which version you use, the market is either still fine, or

596
00:32:12,717 --> 00:32:14,817
getting close to the danger zone.

597
00:32:14,817 --> 00:32:16,917
One caution about the classic version:

598
00:32:16,917 --> 00:32:20,442
earnings tend to grow with inflation, so an earnings yield is closer to a

599
00:32:20,442 --> 00:32:25,030
real return, yet it gets compared with a bond yield that includes inflation.

600
00:32:25,030 --> 00:32:28,842
That's why the CAPE version, measured against real yields, is the more careful

601
00:32:28,842 --> 00:32:29,605
one.

602
00:32:29,605 --> 00:32:32,155
Either way, the direction is the same.

603
00:32:32,155 --> 00:32:36,342
Earnings are strong, but rates are squeezing what investors will pay for them.

604
00:32:40,582 --> 00:32:43,582
Timmer ties it all together with what he calls his clock.

605
00:32:43,582 --> 00:32:45,457
Up means earnings are rising;

606
00:32:45,457 --> 00:32:47,032
down, falling.

607
00:32:47,032 --> 00:32:49,720
Left means financial conditions are easing:

608
00:32:49,720 --> 00:32:51,257
money getting cheaper.

609
00:32:51,257 --> 00:32:52,832
Right means tightening.

610
00:32:52,832 --> 00:32:56,016
Markets tend to travel around it clockwise.

611
00:32:56,016 --> 00:32:59,678
From nine to twelve o'clock, earnings are rising and money is easy:

612
00:32:59,678 --> 00:33:01,741
both engines push prices up.

613
00:33:01,741 --> 00:33:05,366
From twelve to three, earnings are still rising, but money is tightening,

614
00:33:05,366 --> 00:33:07,591
so the multiple pushes the other way.

615
00:33:07,591 --> 00:33:09,966
From three to six, both turn against you.

616
00:33:09,966 --> 00:33:14,291
And from six to nine, earnings are still falling, but easier money lets prices

617
00:33:14,291 --> 00:33:15,766
recover early.

618
00:33:15,766 --> 00:33:20,028
Timmer's clock has been moving from the nine-to-twelve quadrant into twelve-to-three:

619
00:33:20,028 --> 00:33:24,166
rising earnings, held back by tightening financial conditions.

620
00:33:24,166 --> 00:33:28,091
That is this whole video, in one picture.

621
00:33:28,224 --> 00:33:30,374
We can draw the clock with real data.

622
00:33:30,374 --> 00:33:31,224
Up the side:

623
00:33:31,224 --> 00:33:33,637
how fast earnings have grown over the past year.

624
00:33:33,637 --> 00:33:34,374
Across:

625
00:33:34,374 --> 00:33:37,749
how much the real ten-year yield has changed over the past year, a simple

626
00:33:37,749 --> 00:33:39,112
gauge of tightening.

627
00:33:39,112 --> 00:33:41,224
Here's 2020 to 2022:

628
00:33:41,224 --> 00:33:44,499
earnings collapsed while rates fell, then earnings roared back while money

629
00:33:44,499 --> 00:33:48,474
stayed easy, then rates shot up while earnings were still rising, and finally

630
00:33:48,474 --> 00:33:49,887
earnings rolled over.

631
00:33:49,887 --> 00:33:51,637
A full clockwise loop.

632
00:33:51,637 --> 00:33:56,049
2016 to 2018 traced a smaller loop the same way.

633
00:33:56,049 --> 00:33:59,599
And here's this cycle, up to the middle of 2026:

634
00:33:59,599 --> 00:34:02,274
earnings accelerating in the upper left.

635
00:34:02,274 --> 00:34:06,174
Since then, real yields have jumped more than a point, pushing the market

636
00:34:06,174 --> 00:34:08,712
to the right, into twelve to three.

637
00:34:08,712 --> 00:34:15,062
Timmer says the market keeps following the 2017 to 2018 and the 2021 to 2022

638
00:34:15,062 --> 00:34:17,899
playbooks closely.

639
00:34:18,024 --> 00:34:20,837
It's worth remembering what those playbooks looked like.

640
00:34:20,837 --> 00:34:25,949
In 2017 and 2018, a tax-cut earnings boom ran into a Fed that was raising

641
00:34:25,949 --> 00:34:28,374
rates and shrinking its balance sheet.

642
00:34:28,374 --> 00:34:32,237
Late in 2018, stocks fell nearly twenty percent.

643
00:34:32,237 --> 00:34:37,974
In 2021 and 2022, a reopening earnings boom ran into a Fed that pivoted hard

644
00:34:37,974 --> 00:34:43,274
to tightening, and 2022 brought a bear market of about twenty-five percent.

645
00:34:43,274 --> 00:34:47,474
Analogs aren't forecasts, and Timmer doesn't call for a bear market.

646
00:34:47,474 --> 00:34:51,549
But in both, the twelve to three phase is where rates won the tug of war for

647
00:34:51,549 --> 00:34:53,799
a while.

648
00:34:53,974 --> 00:34:55,862
Finally, the biggest picture.

649
00:34:55,862 --> 00:35:01,499
Here's the S&P 500 since 1900, after inflation, on a log scale.

650
00:35:01,499 --> 00:35:05,899
Long stretches of strong gains, secular bull markets, alternate with long

651
00:35:05,899 --> 00:35:07,837
stretches of going nowhere.

652
00:35:07,837 --> 00:35:14,862
From 1949 to 1966, and from 1982 to 2000, prices rose ten to twelve percent

653
00:35:14,862 --> 00:35:19,499
a year after inflation, against a long-run average of about three.

654
00:35:19,499 --> 00:35:24,049
By Timmer's count, which he admits is a minority view, the current secular

655
00:35:24,049 --> 00:35:26,474
bull began in 2009.

656
00:35:26,474 --> 00:35:31,112
That makes it about seventeen years old, about as long as the last two, and

657
00:35:31,112 --> 00:35:35,157
he thinks it's in the final innings of above-average returns.

658
00:35:35,157 --> 00:35:37,057
Which leads to his closing line:

659
00:35:37,057 --> 00:35:40,707
consider harvesting those betas while the sun is shining.

660
00:35:40,707 --> 00:35:44,695
Beta is the return you get just from being exposed to the market.

661
00:35:44,695 --> 00:35:49,232
Harvesting it means collecting those gains, and rebalancing, while conditions

662
00:35:49,232 --> 00:35:53,607
are still good, rather than assuming the best returns will last forever.

663
00:35:55,272 --> 00:35:57,759
Let's put the whole argument back together.

664
00:35:57,759 --> 00:36:01,972
The price of the market is earnings times a multiple, and the multiple depends

665
00:36:01,972 --> 00:36:04,447
on interest rates, through r minus g.

666
00:36:04,447 --> 00:36:05,534
Pulling up:

667
00:36:05,534 --> 00:36:10,509
an earnings boom, with growth around thirty percent, record margins, and companies

668
00:36:10,509 --> 00:36:14,647
still beating estimates, although growth this fast is usually close to its

669
00:36:14,647 --> 00:36:15,772
peak.

670
00:36:15,772 --> 00:36:16,734
Pulling down:

671
00:36:16,734 --> 00:36:21,209
a rising cost of capital, driven by the cost of the government's debt, fewer

672
00:36:21,209 --> 00:36:25,747
price-blind buyers, AI's hunger for capital, and convexity hedging in the

673
00:36:25,747 --> 00:36:30,409
mortgage market, with a Fed that has one blunt instrument for all of it.

674
00:36:30,409 --> 00:36:31,484
The result:

675
00:36:31,484 --> 00:36:36,497
an index going sideways, and a stealth correction underneath.

676
00:36:36,672 --> 00:36:38,209
So what would break the stalemate?

677
00:36:38,209 --> 00:36:39,522
First, rates:

678
00:36:39,522 --> 00:36:43,134
if the pressure from the bond market eases, Timmer thinks the indexes could

679
00:36:43,134 --> 00:36:45,047
easily pick up speed again.

680
00:36:45,047 --> 00:36:47,997
Second, the earnings season starting in mid-October:

681
00:36:47,997 --> 00:36:50,697
whether the boom keeps beating expectations.

682
00:36:50,697 --> 00:36:52,072
Third, breadth:

683
00:36:52,072 --> 00:36:55,597
whether the average stock recovers, or the stealth correction spreads to the

684
00:36:55,597 --> 00:36:56,572
giants.

685
00:36:56,572 --> 00:37:01,647
And the calendar, as the seasonally weak stretch comes to an end.

686
00:37:01,780 --> 00:37:07,305
This video explained one strategist's view, as of early October 2026, and

687
00:37:07,305 --> 00:37:09,355
checked it against public data.

688
00:37:09,355 --> 00:37:11,268
It isn't investment advice.

689
00:37:11,268 --> 00:37:12,505
Thanks for watching.
